    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d918">

                <pb facs="0855" n="847" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg847"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/586: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-03-09T15:00:00+09:00">March 9, 1940—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 9—3:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>161. Chungking 113, March 4, 1 p.m.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d918-f1"/> Tientsin
                    silver. My British colleague informs me that on March 4 he handed to the Vice
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs a statement of the changes desired by the Chinese
                    Government in the proposed Anglo-Japanese agreement with regard to the Tientsin
                    silver and that “Tani’s immediate reaction was to reject the amendments
                    practically <hi rendition="#italic">in toto</hi>”. Craigie is to let me have
                    Tani’s reasoned observations when received.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping for Chungking, Tientsin and Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
